##gff-version 3
#!gff-spec-version 1.21
#!processor NCBI annotwriter
#!genome-build ViralMultiSegProj15521
#!genome-build-accession NCBI_Assembly:GCF_000865725.1
#
# ============================================================================
# PR8 annotation for data/reference/pHW2000_PR8-N_A.fa
#
# Derived from NCBI RefSeq GCF_000865725.1 (NC_002016-NC_002023,
# A/Puerto Rico/8/1934), with two local modifications:
#
#   1. seqids renamed from accessions to segment names
#   2. HA coordinates corrected against GenBank EF467821.1
#
# Both are recorded below, because both are the kind of edit that looks
# arbitrary a year later.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Why the seqids are segment names and not accessions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SNPer requires one name to be identical across every artifact:
#
#     reference FASTA headers == GFF seqids == primer BED chrom
#         == consensus record IDs == meta.segment
#
# Liftoff transfers annotations by seqid, `samtools ampliconclip` matches
# primer BED chrom against each read's reference name, and the split-by-
# segment step keys on reference name. If these drift, annotation transfer,
# primer trimming and the coordinate lift-back all misfire silently.
#
# pHW2000_PR8-N_A.fa is a DNASTAR export whose headers are segment names
# (PB2, PB1, PA, HA, NP, N_A, M, NS) and which carries no accession, so the
# GFF is what had to move. NA is spelled "N_A" to match the FASTA.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Why HA differs from RefSeq
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reference is derived from ATCC VR-1469 stock, which matches GenBank
# EF467821.1 (assembly GCA_037915385.1, "passage level ExMDCK6") -- not the
# RefSeq PR8 lineage. PR8 is not a single sequence; separately passaged
# laboratory stocks have measurably diverged, and this one is 3 nt shorter
# in HA than NC_002017.1:
#
#                     5'UTR      CDS       3'UTR    total   protein
#     this reference    32   +   1698   +    45   =  1775    565 aa
#     NC_002017.1       32   +   1701   +    45   =  1778    566 aa
#
# Both UTRs are intact at their canonical lengths, the ATG at position 33 is
# the first ATG in the sequence, and there is a single clean TGA at codon 566
# with no internal stop. The three missing nucleotides are one in-frame codon
# deleted from inside the CDS -- real lineage sequence, not a truncated
# assembly (which would have eaten a UTR or shifted the frame).
#
# Comparing HA sub-features against EF467821.1 localizes it to HA1, the
# antigenic head domain: the signal peptide is identical (33..83, aa 1-17),
# HA1 is one residue shorter (aa 18-343 vs 18-344), and HA2 is the same
# length shifted 3 nt earlier. The exact codon is not pinned down.
#
# What the uncorrected annotation did wrong: CDS 33..1733 overran the real
# stop codon by one codon. Nothing errored -- nextclade kept the CDS and iVar
# accepted the GFF -- but iVar annotated variants at 1731-1733 as a missense
# D->A in HA when those positions are 3' UTR and never translated. Verified
# by running `ivar variants` over both annotations on a synthetic variant at
# position 1732: the corrected file reports GFF_FEATURE=NA, as it should.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why RefSeq was corrected rather than replaced by GCA_037915385.1
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GCA_037915385.1 matches this reference exactly (all 8 segment lengths, all
# 11 CDS translate cleanly) and was used as the authority for every corrected
# value below. It was not adopted wholesale because:
#
#   - it has no PA-X; that submission predates PA-X's characterization,
#     while RefSeq annotates it at 25..597+599..784 (validates clean)
#   - it names NEP "NS2", the legacy name
#   - its protein IDs differ (NP_040980.1 vs ABO21709.1), which would change
#     every iVar GFF_FEATURE string in published variants/ output
#   - its seqids are accessions, so the rename in (1) would be needed anyway
#
# Every non-HA CDS coordinate is already identical between the two files.
# HA was the only disagreement, so HA is the only thing changed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exact HA changes, applied on top of the seqid rename (all sourced from EF467821.1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ##sequence-region HA          1 1778     ->  1 1775
#   region                        1..1778    ->  1..1775
#   region ID           NC_002017.1:1..1778  ->  HA:1..1775
#   gene                          33..1733   ->  33..1730
#   CDS                           33..1733   ->  33..1730
#   signal_peptide_region_of_CDS  33..83         unchanged
#   mature_protein_region_of_CDS  84..1064   ->  84..1061    (ID :18..344  -> :18..343)
#   mature_protein_region_of_CDS  1065..1730 ->  1062..1727  (ID :345..566 -> :344..565)
#
# The region ID dropped the accession rather than becoming NC_002017.1:1..1775,
# because this HA is not a subrange of NC_002017.1 -- it is a different lineage's
# sequence. Naming EF467821.1 there would assert a base-for-base identity that
# has not been checked (only lengths and CDS validity were). Provenance lives in
# this header; the ID only has to be unique within the file. The other seven
# segments keep their accession IDs, which are still accurate.
#
# Known remaining wart: the HA sub-features use Parent=gene-FLUAVs4gp1 rather
# than Parent=cds-NP_040980.1. That is a workaround for Liftoff, not a
# transcription error -- restore the correct cds- parent only once Liftoff is
# removed from the pipeline, since undoing it earlier re-breaks Liftoff.
#
# Conner Copeland, 2026-08-25
# ============================================================================
#
